@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Simple composite-structure view — a Publisher wired to a Subscriber inside one structured classifier, with ports on the boundary.
'   usecase:      Teach structured classifier frame, parts (roles), connectors, and ports.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       Messaging
' ============================================================================
title Example Composite Structure - Simple Published/Subscribed

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' A COMPOSITE STRUCTURE diagram shows the INTERNAL parts of a classifier and
' how they are wired together.  Key concepts:
'   - STRUCTURED CLASSIFIER: the enclosing frame (here EventChannel).
'   - PART (role): a typed slot owned by the classifier, drawn as a box
'     labelled "roleName : Type".  A part is NOT an instance — it is a
'     placeholder that will be filled by instances at runtime.
'   - CONNECTOR: a line between parts that lets them communicate.
'   - PORT: a typed interaction point on the BOUNDARY of the classifier;
'     external clients connect to ports, and ports are wired to internal
'     parts by delegation connectors.
' PlantUML has no first-class composite-structure syntax, so we emulate it
' with a `component`/`rectangle` frame, `port`/`interface` pins, and a note
' explaining the semantics.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rectangle "EventChannel" <<structuredClassifier>> as channel {
  ' PORT on the boundary — external producers/consumers attach here.
  port "in events : IEventSink"  as pIn
  port "out events : IEventSource" as pOut

  ' TWO PARTS (roles) inside the classifier.
  component "publisher : Publisher" as pub
  component "subscriber : Subscriber" as sub

  ' CONNECTOR wiring the two parts together.
  pub --> sub : dispatch

  ' DELEGATION CONNECTORS from boundary ports to internal parts.
  pIn --> pub
  sub --> pOut
}

note right of channel
  <b>How to read this diagram</b>
  • The rectangle IS the classifier
    "EventChannel" (a structured type).
  • publisher & subscriber are PARTS:
    typed slots, not instances.
  • pub --> sub is a CONNECTOR
    (parts talk over it).
  • in/out are PORTS on the boundary.
  • pIn --> pub is a DELEGATION
    connector: a message sent to the
    port is forwarded to the part.
end note

@enduml
